---
/**
 * The front page: what this is, in the order a provider asks it.
 *
 * Every number on this page comes from the proving ground rather than from
 * marketing, and the evidence page says how each was measured. Where a number
 * is not met yet, the evidence page says that too.
 */
const ladder = [
  ["1. Tell me what I am about to break", "nothing", "On any pull request, exactly what will break for the callers you already have, by endpoint and by field"],
  ["2. Put it in CI", "a CI step", "The same, on every pull request, as a check that fails"],
  ["3. Write the changes down", "text files in your repository", "The check can tell a deliberate break from an accident"],
  ["4. Add the translator", "a dependency, two lines", "Old callers keep working, unchanged"],
  ["5. Release properly", "a signing key", "A signed, reproducible record of every change"],
  ["6. Turn old versions off", "a usage counter", "Evidence for when it is safe"],
];
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Invariant - change your API without breaking anyone</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Invariant reads what your API release changes, blocks what it cannot serve, keeps old callers working, and sends your customers the edit."
    />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="bar">
      <a class="brand" href="/">Invariant</a>
      <nav>
        <a href="/quickstart">Docs</a>
        <a href="/evidence">Evidence</a>
        <a href="#pricing">Pricing</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/InvariantApp/Invariant">GitHub</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="hero">
        <h1>Change your API without breaking anyone.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Invariant reads what your release changes, refuses what it cannot serve, keeps
          every old caller working exactly as before, and sends each of your customers
          the edit as a pull request.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="primary" href="/quickstart">Start with the check</a>
          <a class="secondary" href="/evidence">See the evidence</a>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          The first rung installs nothing and touches nothing. It runs in your own CI and
          tells you what your next release breaks.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The problem is not that APIs change</h2>
        <p>
          It is that the cost lands on people who did not make the decision. A provider
          changes a field, and every integration built on it stops working at a moment
          nobody chose. The usual answers are to freeze the API, to write a migration
          guide that nothing can check or run, or to spend years building a versioning
          system in-house, which is what Stripe, Intercom and Keygen each did.
        </p>
        <p>
          Invariant is that third option as a product, and one thing none of them have:
          it also sends the change to your customers, already made.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How it works</h2>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            <h3>You change your API and open a pull request, as normal.</h3>
            <p>
              Invariant compares the two descriptions, works out what moved, and drafts a
              small file naming each change. You read it and correct it; nothing is
              assumed.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>The release is blocked until what you wrote down explains everything.</h3>
            <p>
              The changes are applied to the old description, and the result is compared
              with the new one. Anything left over is a change nobody wrote down, and the
              gate stops there. This one check is what makes the rest trustworthy.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>From that one file, two things are produced that cannot disagree.</h3>
            <p>
              A translator that runs inside your own service, so old callers keep working;
              and an automatic code edit for each customer who connected a repository,
              delivered as a draft pull request with an explanation of who confirmed it.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Nobody installs someone else's code in their request path on a promise</h2>
        <p>
          So you do not have to. Adoption is a ladder, and every rung is worth having on
          its own. The only rung that touches live traffic is the fourth.
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Step</th><th>What you install</th><th>What you get</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {ladder.map(([step, install, gain]) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{step}</td>
                <td><code>{install}</code></td>
                <td>{gain}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <section id="pricing">
        <h2>Pricing</h2>
        <div class="plans">
          <div class="plan">
            <h3>Free</h3>
            <p class="price">$0</p>
            <p>
              The check and the CI gate, on as many APIs and as many releases as you like,
              for ever. This is the whole first three rungs of the ladder.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="plan">
            <h3>Team</h3>
            <p class="price">Metered</p>
            <p>
              Everything above, plus the translator, signed releases, usage by contract
              and consumer, and migration pull requests. Metered on old contracts served
              and consumers migrated, so it costs nothing while nothing is old.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="plan">
            <h3>Enterprise</h3>
            <p class="price">Annual</p>
            <p>
              Single sign-on, SCIM, audit export, region pinning and a self-hosted
              install. Invoiced annually.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          A caller that can never be migrated, such as a mobile app already in someone's
          pocket, can be pinned and is not billed as an old contract.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What it does not do</h2>
        <p>
          GraphQL, gRPC, batch and RPC endpoints, and file uploads on an adapted endpoint
          are out of scope, and say so rather than half working. gRPC-JSON transcoding is
          in scope, because it is JSON. Everything else that is not built yet is listed on
          the <a href="/evidence">evidence page</a>, with what measures it.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer>
      <p>
        Apache-2.0. Everything that runs in your CI or your request path is open source
        and on npm; the hosted service is what is sold.
      </p>
      <nav>
        <a href="/quickstart">Docs</a>
        <a href="/evidence">Evidence</a>
        <a href="/security">Security</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/InvariantApp/Invariant">GitHub</a>
      </nav>
    </footer>

    <style>
      :root {
        --ink: #14161a;
        --muted: #5b626e;
        --line: #e4e7ec;
        --paper: #ffffff;
        --sunken: #f7f8fa;
        --accent: #2f5bd7;
      }
      @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        :root {
          --ink: #eceef2;
          --muted: #9aa2b1;
          --line: #262a33;
          --paper: #101319;
          --sunken: #171b22;
          --accent: #8ba7f5;
        }
      }
      * { box-sizing: border-box; }
      html { -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; }
      body {
        margin: 0;
        background: var(--paper);
        color: var(--ink);
        font: 16px/1.65 ui-sans-serif, system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
      }
      a { color: var(--accent); }
      code {
        font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
        font-size: 0.875em;
      }
      .bar {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: space-between;
        gap: 1rem;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
      }
      .brand {
        font-weight: 650;
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
        color: var(--ink);
        text-decoration: none;
      }
      .bar nav, footer nav { display: flex; gap: 1.25rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
      .bar nav a, footer nav a { font-size: 0.9375rem; text-decoration: none; }
      .bar nav a:hover, footer nav a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
      main { max-width: 56rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.25rem 4rem; }
      section { padding: 3rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
      section:last-of-type { border-bottom: 0; }
      .hero { padding-top: 4.5rem; }
      h1 {
        font-size: clamp(2rem, 5.5vw, 3.25rem);
        line-height: 1.1;
        letter-spacing: -0.025em;
        margin: 0 0 1rem;
      }
      h2 {
        font-size: clamp(1.375rem, 3vw, 1.75rem);
        letter-spacing: -0.015em;
        margin: 0 0 1rem;
      }
      h3 { font-size: 1.0625rem; margin: 0 0 0.375rem; }
      p { margin: 0 0 1rem; max-width: 46rem; }
      .lead { font-size: 1.1875rem; color: var(--muted); }
      .note { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9375rem; }
      .actions { display: flex; gap: 0.75rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 1.75rem 0 1rem; }
      .actions a {
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        height: 2.75rem;
        padding: 0 1.125rem;
        border-radius: 0.5rem;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-weight: 550;
      }
      .primary { background: var(--accent); color: #fff; }
      .secondary { border: 1px solid var(--line); color: var(--ink); }
      .steps { margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; counter-reset: step; }
      .steps li {
        position: relative;
        padding: 0 0 0 2.75rem;
        margin: 0 0 1.75rem;
        counter-increment: step;
      }
      .steps li::before {
        content: counter(step);
        position: absolute;
        left: 0;
        top: 0.0625rem;
        width: 1.875rem;
        height: 1.875rem;
        border-radius: 999px;
        background: var(--sunken);
        border: 1px solid var(--line);
        display: grid;
        place-items: center;
        font-size: 0.875rem;
        color: var(--muted);
      }
      table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.9375rem; }
      th, td {
        text-align: left;
        padding: 0.625rem 1rem 0.625rem 0;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
        vertical-align: top;
      }
      th { font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--muted); font-weight: 550; }
      .plans {
        display: grid;
        gap: 1rem;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
      }
      .plan {
        border: 1px solid var(--line);
        border-radius: 0.75rem;
        padding: 1.25rem;
        background: var(--sunken);
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
      }
      .plan p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
      .price { font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 650; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
      footer {
        border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
        padding: 2rem 1.25rem 3rem;
        color: var(--muted);
        font-size: 0.9375rem;
      }
      footer p { max-width: 46rem; margin: 0 auto 1rem; }
      footer nav { max-width: 46rem; margin: 0 auto; }
      @media (max-width: 40rem) {
        section { padding: 2.25rem 0; }
        .hero { padding-top: 2.5rem; }
      }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
